      "explain": "OneDrive compresses its own log folders, even when OneDrive is never signed in (observed on a clean Windows 11 26H2 Pro install, build 26300)." },
    { "id": "compressed-windows-logs", "category": "Compressed", "under": ["%SystemRoot%\\INF", "%SystemRoot%\\Logs", "%SystemRoot%\\Panther"], "origin": "trace", "confidence": "observed",
      "explain": "Setup and servicing logs (setupapi, CBS, Panther) that Windows compresses itself (observed on a clean Windows 11 26H2 Pro install, build 26300)." },
    { "id": "compressed-windows", "category": "Compressed", "under": ["%SystemRoot%"], "origin": "unknown", "confidence": "assumed",
      "explain": "NTFS compression inside the Windows folder. Some components and cleanup tasks compress old data (e.g. in WinSxS)." },
    { "id": "compressed-default", "category": "Compressed", "origin": "custom", "confidence": "assumed",
      "explain": "NTFS compression (the blue name in Explorer). New files below a compressed folder are compressed too." },
    { "id": "sparse-default", "category": "Sparse", "origin": "trace", "confidence": "assumed",
      "explain": "Sparse file — an NTFS feature for files that are large on paper but mostly empty. Long runs of zeros are not stored: NTFS only notes \"zeros from here to here\", so the file reports its full size but takes only the space of what was really written (the details show both). Programs mark files sparse on purpose, usually databases that reserve room in advance: BITS (qmgr.db), ESE databases (*.edb), the font cache, virtual disks, download managers. Harmless and normal; nothing to do." },
    { "id": "objectid-default", "category": "ObjectId", "origin": "trace", "confidence": "documented",
      "explain": "An NTFS object ID, created by Distributed Link Tracking when a shortcut or OLE link points at the file, so the link can be repaired if the file moves. A trace of usage, not configuration." },
    { "id": "orphan-default", "category": "Orphan", "origin": "custom", "confidence": "documented",
      "explain": "" },
    { "id": "longpath-default", "category": "LongPath", "origin": "trace", "confidence": "documented",
      "explain": "The path exceeds 259 characters (MAX_PATH). Explorer, older tools and scripts that do not use \\\\?\\ paths or the LongPathsEnabled setting cannot open it. Typical with node_modules or deep backups." },
    { "id": "unreadable-paging", "category": "Unreadable",
      "under": ["?:\\pagefile.sys", "?:\\swapfile.sys", "?:\\hiberfil.sys", "?:\\DumpStack.log.tmp", "?:\\DumpStack.log"],
      "origin": "stock", "confidence": "documented",
      "explain": "Held open exclusively by the memory manager while Windows runs: the paging file, the swap file for packaged apps, the hibernation file, or the crash-dump stack log. No process can open it — not even with backup privilege — so its metadata cannot be read. Expected on every running system." },
    { "id": "unreadable-default", "category": "Unreadable", "origin": "unknown", "confidence": "documented",
      "explain": "" }
  ],

