  "items": [
    {"id": "mapdata", "path": "%ProgramData%\\Microsoft\\MapData", "explain": "Storage for Windows' offline maps, owned by the MapsBroker service (\"Downloaded Maps Manager\"), which downloads and updates map regions for apps. Removing the Maps app leaves the service and this folder behind.", "howReversible": "Stop it from running (admin PowerShell):\nSet-Service MapsBroker -StartupType Disabled\n\nRestore:\nSet-Service MapsBroker -StartupType Manual", "howFinal": "After the service is disabled (or it recreates the folder):\nRemove-Item 'C:\\ProgramData\\Microsoft\\MapData' -Recurse -Force"},
    {"id": "devicesync", "path": "%ProgramData%\\Microsoft\\DeviceSync", "explain": "Created from the Windows installation image. Its ACL is open by design: Everyone has full control and it carries a Low integrity label, so even sandboxed low-integrity processes may write here. Probably part of portable-device sync (Windows Portable Devices / MTP, as used by media players) — assumed, not documented. Usually empty when nothing syncs devices."},
    {"id": "windowsapps-package-cache", "name": "Cache", "under": ["%ProgramData%\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsApps"], "explain": "Per-version cache of an app package: four hive-shaped files (<hash>.dat, <hash>_COM15.dat and its .LOG1/.LOG2), one set per app version that ever ran. They are written as SYSTEM with Administrators limited to read and execute — which is why an administrator's delete is refused — and older versions are not removed by whatever writes them. Which component writes them is not recorded (assumed: the app's installer or update step).", "howFinal": "Delete old versions (admin; Administrators own the files, so they can grant themselves full control first). Keep the version that runs:\n$root = 'C:\\ProgramData\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsApps'\n$keep = '<folder of the running version>'\nGet-ChildItem $root -Directory | Where-Object Name -ne $keep | ForEach-Object {\n  icacls $_.FullName /grant '*S-1-5-32-544:F' /T /C /Q | Out-Null\n  Remove-Item $_.FullName -Recurse -Force\n}"},
    {"id": "diagnosis", "path": "%ProgramData%\\Microsoft\\Diagnosis", "explain": "Working folder of Windows diagnostic data (telemetry), mainly the DiagTrack service (\"Connected User Experiences and Telemetry\"): EventStore.db and EventTranscript (what the Diagnostic Data Viewer shows), DownloadedSettings and DownloadedScenarios (configuration pushed down), ETLLogs (the DiagTrack autologger), FeedbackHub, SoftLanding (tips and first-run content). Nothing in Windows depends on this data to work.", "howReversible": "Settings → Privacy & security → Diagnostics & feedback: lowest level available (\"Required\" on Pro).\nStop the service:  Set-Service DiagTrack -StartupType Disabled      (restore: Automatic)\nUpdates may set parts of it back; check after patch days.", "howFinal": "Settings → Privacy & security → Diagnostics & feedback → \"Delete diagnostic data\", or delete the contents of this folder (not the folder) with the service stopped."},
    {"id": "perceptionsimulation", "path": "%SystemRoot%\\System32\\PerceptionSimulation", "explain": "Perception simulation of Windows Mixed Reality: lets developers fake head, hand and controller input without a headset. Microsoft removed Windows Mixed Reality from Windows 11 starting with 24H2; on machines upgraded from earlier versions this folder can remain. No optional feature or capability lists it as removable (assumed: a remnant)."},
    {"id": "winsxs", "path": "%SystemRoot%\\WinSxS", "explain": "Windows' component store. Most of what Windows runs lives here and is hard-linked into System32 and the other system folders, so Explorer counts it twice. Updates keep the old version of each component they replace, so an update can be uninstalled; Windows' scheduled servicing task removes old versions 30 days after they were superseded. The clean-up is offered below.", "howFinal": "With /ResetBase (installed updates can no longer be uninstalled):\nDISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /StartComponentCleanup /ResetBase", "how": "Without /ResetBase — the same step the scheduled task runs:\nDISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /StartComponentCleanup"},
    {"id": "setupcln", "path": "%SystemRoot%\\System32\\LogFiles\\setupcln", "explain": "Logs of Windows Setup's clean-up — the step that removes what an upgrade leaves behind (Windows.old, temporary setup files), run by Disk Cleanup (\"Previous Windows installation(s)\") or by Windows itself after an upgrade: setupact.log (everything), setuperr.log (errors), diagerr.xml and diagwrn.xml (errors and warnings as structured diagnostics). Each file grants the account the clean-up ran under; the next run rewrites them."},
    {"id": "fontcache-fonts", "path": "%SystemRoot%\\ServiceProfiles\\LocalService\\AppData\\Local\\FontCache\\Fonts", "explain": "Cache of fonts loaded on demand by the Windows Font Cache Service (FontCache, runs as LOCAL SERVICE): Windows can fetch some fonts from Microsoft only when an app asks for one. Files are sparse — only the pieces actually needed are downloaded — and fontset-*.json is the catalogue. Harmless to delete; rebuilt when a font is needed again.", "howFinal": "Stop-Service FontCache -Force\nRemove-Item 'C:\\Windows\\ServiceProfiles\\LocalService\\AppData\\Local\\FontCache\\Fonts\\*' -Force\nStart-Service FontCache"},
    {"id": "hyperv-snapshots-cache", "path": "%ProgramData%\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Hyper-V\\Snapshots Cache", "explain": "One cached checkpoint configuration (<GUID> folder and <GUID>.vmcx) per existing checkpoint of any registered VM, wherever the VM's own files are. vmms creates and removes the entries with the checkpoints; an entry without a matching checkpoint would be an anomaly."},
    {"id": "firefox-default-browser-agent", "name": "Firefox Default Browser Agent *", "under": ["%SystemRoot%\\System32\\Tasks\\Mozilla"], "explain": "A scheduled task Firefox registers for the account that installs it (the Default Browser Agent). It runs once a day as that account, checks which browser is the default and reports it to Mozilla's telemetry; some versions also prompt to make Firefox the default again. The hex suffix is derived from the Firefox install path, so each installation gets its own task. If the account it runs as is deleted, it never runs again.", "howFinal": "Remove it (admin PowerShell):\nUnregister-ScheduledTask -TaskPath '\\Mozilla\\' -TaskName '<the task name>' -Confirm:$false\n\nKeep Firefox from registering it again (policy, DWORD):\nHKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Mozilla\\Firefox  DisableDefaultBrowserAgent = 1"},
    {"id": "mozilla-programdata-update", "path": "%ProgramData%\\Mozilla-*", "explain": "Firefox's update folder (the name ends in a GUID). Firefox's updater and the Mozilla Maintenance Service use it to stage updates. So that updates can run without an administrator, Firefox makes it writable for ordinary users by design; the Maintenance Service, running as SYSTEM, then installs what is staged there. Removing the Maintenance Service leaves this folder in place; Firefox recreates it while installed.", "how": "If Firefox is uninstalled, delete the folder. If Firefox stays, it comes back. To stop the SYSTEM service path (updates then ask for an administrator): uninstall \"Mozilla Maintenance Service\" in Settings → Apps, or set the policy\nHKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Mozilla\\Firefox  DisableAppUpdate = 1   (no automatic updates at all)"},
    {"id": "mozilla-maintenance-service", "path": "%ProgramFiles(x86)%\\Mozilla Maintenance Service", "explain": "A Windows service Firefox installs (MozillaMaintenance). It runs as SYSTEM and lets an ordinary user's Firefox install updates without an administrator prompt — any user can make it run an update. The design has had privilege-escalation bugs; removing it is a reasonable choice. Without it Firefox still updates, but each update asks for an administrator.", "how": "Settings → Apps → \"Mozilla Maintenance Service\" → Uninstall."},
    {"id": "registry-txr-logs", "name": "*.dat{*}.TM*", "under": ["%ProfilesDir%"], "explain": "Registry transaction log of the hive next to it (NTUSER.DAT or UsrClass.dat). The GUID in its name is version 1: the moment it was made and the kind of network adapter of the machine that made it are under Known. Windows manages these files itself."},
    {
      "id": "machinekeys-store", "path": "%ProgramData%\\Microsoft\\Crypto\\RSA\\MachineKeys", "origin": "stock", "confidence": "observed",
      "explain": "Machine-wide private keys (CryptoAPI): one file per key container. Certificates of the machine (RDP, Hyper-V guardians, …) keep their keys here, but so does a steady trickle nobody cleans up: a VM with a virtual TPM adds one key per start and one every 5 minutes while it runs. \"Who's below\" shows which service owns how many; thousands under NT SERVICE\\HgClientService point to vTPM VMs.",
      "how": "Count and date them (elevated PowerShell):\n  Get-ChildItem -Force 'C:\\ProgramData\\Microsoft\\Crypto\\RSA\\MachineKeys' -File | Group-Object Length | Sort-Object Count -Descending\nKeys still used by a certificate are never safe to delete. Remove only keys no certificate in Cert:\\LocalMachine uses, and only while no VM with a virtual TPM is running. Administrators have no access to these files: take ownership first (takeown /a + icacls /grant Administrators:F) or delete them with backup semantics."
    },
    {
      "id": "desktop-ini",
      "name": "desktop.ini",
      "origin": "trace",
      "confidence": "documented",
      "showText": true,
      "explain": "Folder customisation file, hidden and marked as system. Explorer reads it to show a folder differently from its real name or look: LocalizedResourceName gives the display name (on a German Windows the folder Documents shows as \"Dokumente\" because of this line), IconResource the icon, and [ViewState] or other sections the view. Windows setup writes it into the known folders (Documents, Desktop, Start Menu…); Explorer writes it when someone changes a folder's icon or name in its properties; OneDrive, Dropbox and other programs write it for their own folders. Deleting it only changes how Explorer shows the folder, never its contents or permissions."
    },
    {
      "id": "profile-defaultuser0",
      "path": "%ProfilesDir%\\defaultuser0",
      "origin": "trace",
      "confidence": "documented",
      "explain": "Temporary account Windows setup (OOBE) uses before the first real account exists - not the template for new accounts (that is C:\\Users\\Default). Setup normally deletes the account when it finishes, but its profile folder often stays behind, sometimes the account too. If the account is gone, the folder is a leftover: nothing uses it, and its SID shows as an orphan."
    },
    {
      "id": "profile-default",
      "path": "%ProfilesDir%\\Default",
      "origin": "stock",
      "confidence": "documented",
      "explain": "Template for new profiles. When an account signs in for the first time, Windows copies this folder to create its profile. Anything placed here ends up in every future profile."
    },
    {
      "id": "wer-store",
      "paths": [
        "%ProgramData%\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WER",
        "%ProgramData%\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WER\\ReportArchive",
        "%ProgramData%\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WER\\ReportQueue",
        "%ProfilesDir%\\*\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WER"
      ],
      "origin": "stock",
      "confidence": "documented",
      "explain": "Windows Error Reporting. Every crash or hang of a program produces a report: ReportQueue holds those not yet sent, ReportArchive those already processed or sent. On a development machine most of them are your own builds crashing. The folders may be emptied by hand; switching reporting off stops new ones.",
      "how": "Switch off Windows Error Reporting (elevated):\n  Group Policy: Computer Configuration > Administrative Templates > Windows Components > Windows Error Reporting > \"Disable Windows Error Reporting\" = Enabled\n  which writes: HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Error Reporting  Disabled = 1 (DWORD)\n  without policy: HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Error Reporting  Disabled = 1 (DWORD)\n  optional: disable the scheduled task \\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Error Reporting\\QueueReporting\nreg add \"HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Error Reporting\" /v Disabled /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f"
    }
  ]
}
